welcome to the third season of the  official community guide video series  for ARMA 3 my name is lexi and in  cooperation with Bohemia Interactive I'm  creating a series of videos to explain  some of the basics of ARMA 3 if you're  new to the community guides be sure to  also check out the previous episodes  which focus on topics like infantry  combat multiplayer teamwork and  helicopters as with ARMA 3 in general  helicopter usage is a deep topic with  many facets to it today we'll be  expanding on how to use them in combat  more specifically let's look at how to  execute a basic helicopter born air  assault we'll go over this from start to  finish from the initial pickup to the  extraction phase after the missions been  completed the benefits of air assaults  lie on the mobility and flexibility that  they offer helicopters fly fast low and  you can land pretty much anywhere that  you can fit the rotors into with  sufficient helicopters or multiple  flights you can transport a large number  of infantry practically anywhere you  need to in a short period of time as  well as pick them up and reposition them  for new objectives with relative ease  it's a welcomed option considering the  massive terrain sizes of the Altis and  Stratis islands  to start out the person in command of  the mission will be choosing the landing  zones for his elements and breathe them  to the pilots the pilots in turn will  assess these landing zones and adjust  them as necessary based on their own  personal experiences when choosing a  landing zone consider what threats you  are likely to be facing your mission  briefing whether it's a single-player or  multiplayer scenario should give you an  idea of this but remember that it could  be misleading generally speaking you  want to play it safe it's better to walk  a bit further after landing than to  never land at all look for L Z's that  are in positions that mask them from the  most likely directions of enemy fire or  observation when your pilot's are  capable try to pick landing zones close  to cover and concealment such that the  dismounting infantry can immediately  take up protected positions to fight  from once the main landing zone or zones  have been chosen pick at least one  alternate LZ this is done so that in the  event that the primary LZ is unsuitable  for whatever reason  pilots can quickly move to land in an  alternate that's been chosen carefully  in advance instead of as a  spur-of-the-moment decision when flying  large elements such as platoons into an  area picking several different al Z's  and sending different helicopters to  each will help to spread the unit out  and give it more tactical flexibility of  on landing the vast and varied terrain  of Altis and Stratis will make this a  constant topic for evaluation not every  location will have obvious landing zones  and you may have to be rather creative  when the terrain seems stacked against  you there are a few additional  considerations to help ensure a safe  landing the most obvious is having a  gunship escort and prep the LZ in  advance of the transports arriving this  isn't always possible due to mission  design but when available it's a great  way to provide security to the landings  the next best thing is to have a scout  aircraft one which can fly in the LZ and  take a look around shortly before the  transports arrive some transport  aircraft like the Hellcat of powerful  cameras it can observe an area from a  safe distance if you have this sort of  capability use it when these aren't  available a decision must be made about  how to approach the LZ either by flying  directly into it or spending a bit of  time orbiting it to scout it out before  landing choosing the land multiple  aircraft at the same time can result in  unexpected enemy fire dispersing amongst  many targets as well as having many  weapons to turn that fire  whereas flying in single aircraft one at  a time can allow the other aircraft  option  used to wait law firm ha tells these or  provide aerial gunnery support during  the landings once all squads and pilots  have been briefed on their landing zones  and objectives it's time to load up a  troop load in a helicopter is known as a  chalk due to cargo limitations an  element such as a squad may not all fit  into one helo it's important to make  sure that each chalk has a leader in it  such as a squad or a fireteam leader  these leaders are extremely important  they ensure that their chalks loading  the correct helo they notify the pilot  when the chocks loaded and they provide  leadership at the landing zone until  such time as they can merge back into  their parent unit the flight from the  pickup zone to the landing zone must be  carefully planned for avoiding any known  enemy locations is of course a key  element and the altitude and path chosen  will be based unknown as well as  suspected enemy threats flying high  works well in environments where the  threat is primarily based on machine  guns and other such weapons with limited  range and targeting capabilities but it  doesn't work so well when missile  systems are present on the other hand  flying low makes one vulnerable to  machine guns and similar threats but  generally makes it hard for them or  missile systems to engage the aircraft  due to low exposure times and masking  terrain trees and similar of course if  you flying low it's essential to have  good pilots who know their own  capabilities and are able to avoid any  obstacles such as power lines that might  present themselves during low-level  flight  the final approach to the landing zone  requires the utmost and concentration  from the pilots as well as door Gunners  a good LZ approach is done in a smooth  controlled fashion being able to  promptly land without floundering around  is necessary to give any enemies as  little time as possible to react to the  presence of the helicopters note that  pilots have the final say here it can  call for a wave off or alternate LZ if  they deem it necessary the pilots are  the ones who know their capabilities and  can make judgment calls about any  threats or obstacles in the LZ s if they  say they can't make it and they need to  wave off or convert that's how it's  going to be once an aircraft touches  down the pilots the one who calls for  dismount typically by saying go go go  this is due to the fact that only the  pilot truly knows when he's fully landed  if embark troops try to dismount on  their own discretion they may find that  the first step out the door is  unpleasantly long when the troops have  fully dismounted the chaulk leader tells  the pilot he's clear to depart the helos  crew chief will also observe the  interior the aircraft and if he sees  that all the troops depart he tells the  pilot as well  the pilot leaves when either the chaulk  leader or the crew chief tells me is  empty  if the LZ is hot which is to say that  enemy fire is being received during the  landing the crew chief and door gunner  will suppress known or suspected enemy  locations upon landing the whole fire to  avoid hitting the dismounting troops  after they take off they can resume  their fire when taking off pilots  communicate their takeoff directions to  each other in order to avoid mid-air  collisions while this direction should  have been planned for in advance and  briefed accordingly unexpected contact  of the LZ can cause an alternate route  to be decided on the spot once all  troops have been dropped off helos can  either return to base and anticipation  of fairing and reinforcements to  resupply or they can stay in the areas  reconnaissance or light air support this  is based on the nature the threats in  the area of operations so don't expect  the aircraft to stick around with heavy  machine guns vehicles or anti-air  missiles likely to show up in the fight  at some point an extraction will be  required as with the initial landing  this too requires careful execution to  pull off safely when preparing for an  extraction ground forces attempt to move  away from known or suspected them any  threats  searching for a pickup zone that fulfils  the same criteria as the original  landing zone once identified ground  forces move to the pickup zone and  establish security around it ground  leaders establish what order they'll  extract in where the first elements be  picked up staying closest to the pickup  site while the other ones provide  security the pickup zone location is  communicated to the aircraft and if  necessary the ground commander deploys  smoke once the lead aircraft is thirty  seconds out when operating nighttime  conditions infrared strobes lasers and  such can be used to covertly mark  landing zone while chemical lights and  smoke grenades can be combined to give a  very visible landing zone marker when  light discipline is not a concern once  the aircraft's on the deck remember that  for any given chalk the leader will load  in the aircraft last he may have another  person providing security close to the  landed helo while the others load up but  once he knows his chalk is loaded he  communicates this to the pilot so the  speedy liftoff can occur this process is  repeated squad by squad and the aircraft  by aircraft until the pickup zone has  been completely evacuated now when all  said and done you return back to base  after successful mission congratulations  are in order what you've conducted her  participated in wasn't a stage cinematic  event it was a living breathing dynamic  experience it took skill knowledge and  coordination to pull off successfully  this sort of depth is a hallmark of the  ARMA series if you're anything like me  so it keeps you coming back again and  again  [Music]  now if you'd like to know more about  helicopter tactics I'd recommend  checking out my ARMA 3 tackle guide  which you can find on the Bohemia  Interactive store for more community  guide videos be sure to subscribe to the  official ARMA 